ent  quebec receives 6 billion over 5 years  trudeau has been traveling across the  country in recent weeks  making campaign-style pitches for more  affordable child care and  it's all been happening as leaders of  canada's federal parties hit the road  making policy announcements  and seemingly gearing up for an expected  election  our ottawa bureau chief mercedes  stevenson joins me now so mercedes  how close are we to an election being  called welfara  buckle up and get ready to ride the  pandemic election roller coaster because  things are about to kick off after  months of speculation that we've been  talking about  multiple senior sources are telling  global news the election call  is imminent and the writ is expected to  drop  sometime next week that means prime  minister justin trudeau would be taking  down his own minority government  in a bid to come back with a majority  government  but what will that self-imposed election  be about  well the prime minister was asked just  that today  it's becoming clear and obvious to all  the parties in the house of commons that  you were going to call an election  what is the ballot question going to be  mr prime minister  we continue to be focused on delivering  for canadians getting us  through this pandemic making sure that  people continue to get vaccinated  not much of an answer there on the  record pharah but behind the scenes  senior liberals say that they hope  canadians will reward the government's  pandemic performance  and be in a good mood after a second  shot and with all of this  warm southern summer weather the  election is still a risk though while  the liberals are riding high in the  pools  they will need victories in key ridings  where those winds could be  razor thin and the clock is ticking with  rising covet cases and the delta variant  spreading  the window to go to the pools is rapidly  closing  and mercedes it's clear the leader of  the conservative party has his concerns  about an election being called  yeah aaron o'toole has been struggling  to get political traction and attention  and along with the ndp he's accusing the  liberals of  unnecessarily calling an election out of  political self-interest  now is not the time for an election we  can all wait and go to the polls  when it's safe we need to focus on  health and well-being like many families  we want to get past this pandemic  but let's pull together for one more  fight let's beat kovid 19  and have an election when it's safe the  liberals insist that they are having  trouble getting key legislation passed  and note that several provinces have  safely held elections during the  pandemic  and of course as we know the results of  those elections have shown  that in a time of crisis voters  typically stick to the incumbents  vara all right mercedes you're about to  get even busier thank you mercedes  stevenson in ottawa  still ahead alberta's top doctor doubles  down on the protested plan to axe  pandemic restrictions  the dixie fire the largest wildfire in  california has completely gutted a  historic town that dates back to the  gold rush  this is greenville after flames tore  through the area  among the buildings lost an old  sheriff's office saloons and homes  firefighters struggled to drive through  the smoke as strong winds and dry brush  are fueling the flames  the u.s is currently fighting about a  hundred large active fires  about 10 percent of those are in  california  alberta's top doctor is feeling the heat  dina hanshaw is defending her decision  to end the province's pandemic response  plan she has faced  backlash from doctors groups right  across the country since last week's  announcement that alberta would be  scaling down testing and contact tracing  and  would no longer require coveted positive  people to isolate  as heather urux west explains hinshaw  insists the plan  is rooted in science  for the seventh straight day people  gathered in alberta to protest  fearing the province is ending its  pandemic response plan  far too soon we feel very strongly that  we need to continue  to do the testing to do the tracing to  do the isolating  as a bare minimum response to  to a situation that hasn't come to a  close as of  august 16th alberta is scaling back  things like testing contact tracing  and isolation requirements the plan has  prompted a lot of concern  federal health minister patty haideu  sent a letter sunday to her alberta  health counterpart  our most recent modeling for alberta  forecasts a potentially more serious  resurgence in cases in the coming weeks  and months  fueled by the highly transmissible delta  variant  given this epidemiological context i am  seeking to better understand the public  health rationale  and scientific information behind your  government's recent announcements  i feel as i said in my op-ed  very sorry that i didn't communicate in  a way that articulated the rationale  as well as the things that we're going  to continue to do to manage covet 19.  dr dina hinshaw says testing tracing and  isolation work will shift to higher risk  environments vaccine coverage she says  means the health care system is  unlikely to be overwhelmed by covet-19  even as  cases in the province begin to rise i  believe i would not be doing my job  if i kept focused on covid as  the number one risk that we face when i  don't believe it is anymore  with the advent of vaccine we have had a  laser focus on covert for the last year  to the detriment of many other processes  uh you know cancer diagnoses  wound infections um you know heart  surgeries all these types of things  and that that's had a measurable impact  still dr  suman chakrabarti says it feels too soon  to make these changes but hinshaw's  reasoning he says  is scientifically sound question is  how will the virus respond the rest of  the country  will be watching heather eurex west  global news calgary from orphan to  olympian  next the story of a u.s diver and the  unconditional devotion  of his dad  our final story tonight is more than a  journey  it's an inspiration it's about an orphan  from cambodia who dared to dream  defied all the odds and made it to the  olympics  thanks to a special bond with his  american dad  as sean o'shea reports jordan windall is  using his platform  to achieve much more than a perfect dive  for this olympic diver who says he's  afraid of heights  jordan windall is focused on making the  smallest splash possible  at 22 wendell is no stranger to olympic  qualifying  at 12 he was the youngest diver ever to  make the olympic trials  a high achievement considering how his  life started  i was adopted at 18 months from cambodia  the one thing in the world that i wanted  more than anything  was to be a dad jerry wendell adopted  jordan after his parents dropped him off  at the gates of a cambodian orphanage  i was really malnourished at the time i  was really sick we slept in like these  baskets hanging from the ceiling jerry  had just retired from the u.s navy  when the adoption service called to say  they had the perfect little boy to be  his son  the moment i laid eyes on him you know  my heart just  he was my baby for the most part it's  just been him and i  he has sacrificed more than anyone i  know 20 years ago  jerry was not a typical adoptive parent  he's a single gay man  so at the time you know gaining a family  was just super difficult jordan faced  bullying growing up in texas  because of his dad's sexual orientation  and because father and son  don't look alike they wrote a book  together the true story of a boy  sharing their story so i decided to get  a tattoo today i'm gonna get  the cambodian flag how i can be a role  model to other children  and other people in cambodia that don't  have that chance he wants  them to be as proud as he wants all of  us in america to be proud  no matter what i have them in my heart  and i have them with me  the diver competing for two countries  and for the father who made it possible  shawn o'shea global news toronto  what an incredible athlete in every way  that is global national for this  thursday  i'm farah nasser thank you so much for  spending part of your evening with us  tonight's your canada is kempenfelt bay  in barrie ontario we love seeing your  canada so please email us your pictures  at viewers at globalnational.com until  tomorrow  take care of yourselves and each other  you
 

